recipe.rubric:
Grade the map text the way the buyer at {{ account.name }} reads it: the headline, the intro, one line per competitor, the line on {{ account.name }}, then the open space. 10 is a map a sceptical buyer trusts because every line could only be about that company.
Score these, 0 to 10 each:
- grounded: every claim about a company is in its homepage text or its Ken Search description below, and nothing about {{ account.name }} goes beyond its homepage text and the summary. A line that could be about any company in the industry scores low.
- specific: each competitor line names who the company sells to and how it sells, in plain words a person would say out loud, not marketing phrases copied from the homepage.
- readable: short sentences, no jargon, no stacked nouns; the headline and intro say what the map is in one pass.
- open_space: the title names a position on the two axes, and why explains from the inputs both why nobody sits there and what {{ account.name }} already has that fits.
Answer yes or no:
- invented: does any line state a customer, number, result, funding round or product that the inputs below do not support? Quote it.
- weird_word: is there a phrase a person would not say out loud (jargon, marketing adjectives, stacked nouns)? Quote it.
Automatic fail: an em dash or en dash, an exclamation mark, a price, an email address or phone number, a placeholder, or a claim about a company that its homepage text and description below do not support.
Inputs the writer had. Ken Search candidates: {{ data.candidates | json }}
